1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,709
In this lesson, we'll connect
your own sending domain,

2
00:00:03,709 --> 00:00:07,215
so the emails you send from
Kyntlo come from your business,

3
00:00:07,215 --> 00:00:09,474
and land in the inbox instead of spam.

4
00:00:10,356 --> 00:00:12,744
Open Settings, then Email Services.

5
00:00:12,744 --> 00:00:17,112
Right now, Bloom's emails go out
through a shared Kyntlo domain.

6
00:00:17,112 --> 00:00:22,503
That works for testing, but Gmail, Yahoo
and Outlook now expect bulk senders to

7
00:00:22,503 --> 00:00:23,800
prove who they are.

8
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:29,532
A dedicated domain does exactly that, and
keeps your sending reputation yours alone.

9
00:00:30,382 --> 00:00:31,602
Click Add Domain.

10
00:00:31,602 --> 00:00:34,830
Use a subdomain, like
mail.bloomdental.co.uk.

11
00:00:34,830 --> 00:00:39,207
It keeps marketing email separate
from your everyday mailbox,

12
00:00:39,207 --> 00:00:44,230
so if a campaign ever has a bad day,
your normal email isn't affected.

13
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:47,934
Kyntlo now shows the DNS records to add.

14
00:00:47,934 --> 00:00:50,789
DNS is the address book for your domain,

15
00:00:50,789 --> 00:00:55,284
and you change it wherever you
bought the domain, like GoDaddy,

16
00:00:55,284 --> 00:00:56,854
Cloudflare or 123 Reg.

17
00:00:56,854 --> 00:01:00,922
Add each record exactly as
shown, using the copy buttons.

18
00:01:01,772 --> 00:01:04,356
The first two are the most important.

19
00:01:04,356 --> 00:01:08,198
SPF lists who is allowed to
send email for your domain.

20
00:01:08,198 --> 00:01:11,341
DKIM adds a digital
signature to every email,

21
00:01:11,341 --> 00:01:16,509
so inboxes can tell it really came
from you and wasn't changed on the way.

22
00:01:17,357 --> 00:01:21,668
The two MX records handle bounces
and replies for the subdomain.

23
00:01:21,668 --> 00:01:24,902
The CNAME record powers
open and click tracking.

24
00:01:24,902 --> 00:01:29,617
And DMARC tells inboxes what to do
with email that fails these checks.

25
00:01:29,617 --> 00:01:33,322
If you don't have a DMARC
record yet, add this one too.

26
00:01:34,170 --> 00:01:35,009
Short on time?

27
00:01:35,009 --> 00:01:39,623
If your domain is with a supported
provider, click Add records automatically,

28
00:01:39,623 --> 00:01:42,919
sign in to your provider,
and Kyntlo adds them for you.

29
00:01:43,769 --> 00:01:46,166
Once the records are in, click Verify.

30
00:01:46,166 --> 00:01:50,644
Changes can take anything from a few
minutes to a day or two to spread,

31
00:01:50,644 --> 00:01:54,996
so if it doesn't verify straight
away, come back later and try again.

32
00:01:54,996 --> 00:01:58,654
Here, every record checks out,
and the domain is verified.

33
00:01:59,504 --> 00:02:04,840
Back on Email Services, your new domain
is verified and set as the default.

34
00:02:04,840 --> 00:02:10,531
From now on, your campaigns, workflows
and one-to-one emails all go out from it.

35
00:02:10,531 --> 00:02:16,080
If you send very large volumes, a dedicated
IP is also available as an add-on.

36
00:02:16,929 --> 00:02:19,072
One more tab: Email Verification.

37
00:02:19,072 --> 00:02:23,748
Kyntlo can check new addresses before
you send to them, for $0.005 each.

38
00:02:23,748 --> 00:02:28,878
Invalid addresses are skipped, which
protects the reputation you've just built.

39
00:02:29,727 --> 00:02:30,825
Here's a pro tip.

40
00:02:30,825 --> 00:02:33,473
A brand new domain has no reputation yet.

41
00:02:33,473 --> 00:02:38,123
For the first couple of weeks, send
to your most engaged contacts first,

42
00:02:38,123 --> 00:02:40,125
and grow your volume gradually.

43
00:02:40,125 --> 00:02:43,225
Inboxes learn to trust
you much faster that way.

44
00:02:44,078 --> 00:02:46,751
Your emails are ready
to land in the inbox!

45
00:02:46,751 --> 00:02:51,661
Next, we'll connect your other tools with
integrations and the App Marketplace.

46
00:02:51,661 --> 00:02:52,531
See you there.
